WALKING past the corner of Seventh Avenue and Sterling Place in Park Slope, you have a sense that something big, and not altogether pleasant, happened here once.
There, in the middle of one of the city��s hottest – and most historic – residential neighborhoods are two empty lots where stately brownstones once stood.
You can��t put your finger on it exactly. A fire, perhaps, or maybe a building collapse. But all that��s left is a haunting scar – a vague reminder of unfinished business and the gloom of history interrupted.
Locals know it as ��the crash site,�� but outsiders don��t know that they are walking past the site of one of the worst aviation disasters since Wilbur and Orville first took wing.
There, at the corner of Sterling Place and Seventh Avenue, on the snowy morning of Dec. 16, 1960, a United DC-8 en route to the Airport Formerly Known as Idlewild came to rest after colliding with a TWA Constellation over Staten Island.
The TWA plane, sliced clean through by the DC-8��s wing, plummeted to earth, killing all 44 aboard.
The United pilot, who had been 12 miles off course, struggled to make an emergency landing at LaGuardia – but with his wing damaged and one engine buried in the TWA fuselage, he slammed into Park Slope, killing all 83 aboard and six people on the ground.
When the smoke finally cleared on Sterling Place, a gap – in the form of those two empty lots – was left in the neighborhood.
But next month, a developer will start building a five-story apartment building on one of the lots. And this fall, the owner of the other plans to put up a brownstone similar to the one that used to stand there.
Many think the scar will finally be healed.
��I��ve been looking at these lots for 40 years, so this will be better,�� said Henry McCadden, whose funeral parlor at the corner of Sterling and Seventh was destroyed by the plane��s wing.
After the crash, McCadden immediately built a one-story structure, but the squat, ugly building has always had a temporary, Band-Aid feel to it that never erased the memory of the day.
Despite his line of work, McCadden doesn��t think of the corner of Sterling and Seventh as any kind of memorial.
��The pain wears off,�� he said. ��You know, people stop going to cemeteries to pay their respects after a while. That��s how I feel about this spot.��
One of McCadden��s best friends, Albert Layer the butcher, was killed on the ground – but, like the funeral director, Layer��s son didn��t see much point in publicly memorializing the dead.
��Sometimes I think about how he died,�� said Ralph Layer, now 70. ��It was horrible, we had to identify him from his dentures. But I really don��t have any feelings about that site.��
Developer John English also thinks little about the history that crash-landed on his little plot of earth 40 years ago.
