Philadelphia Performing Arts, a String Theory Charter School is not your average high school.
So it makes sense that its building isn't either.
Housed in the former GlaxoSmithKline building at 1600 Vine St. in Philadelphia, the school has eight stories and great views of the Benjamin Franklin Parkway and Logan Square.
Inside, there some hints of its past as a pharmaceutical company's office building -- the most noticeable being on its top floor, where half of it is still in its old configuration. There are white walls, beige carpet and numerous offices, each with a window.
The other half of the floor is the Particle Accelerator, an incubator where startup companies can have office space in exchange to educating students. So far the businesses that are Particle cover everything from fashion to video games.
There are no books at the school, nor a traditional library. Instead there are collaborative spaces where classes can intermingle and work on projects together. Students carry around tablets rather than textbooks. They drink coffee in a student-run cafe where they roast their own beans (which are sold at nearby cafes).
There are some core parts of a school that you'll find at Philadelphia Performing Arts. There's a cafeteria -- only this one is several floors up and looks out on Logan Square. There are classrooms, although some of them contain hydroponic labs and fish tanks full of tilapia that the students are raising. Others may be filled with silk screen printing machines, 3D printers and robots (the latter being made by students). A small theater is around for students to perform in, although they also perform in various theaters around Philadelphia. There's also a 3D character design and animation studio and two dance studios.
